{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A bicycle tire has a pressure of $6\\,\\text{psi}.$ It is being inflated, and the pressure is increasing at a rate of $4t\\,\\text{psi}$ per second, where $t$ is a time, in seconds, since the start of the inflation. The tire has a slow puncture that reduces pressure at a rate of $(t+2)\\,\\text{psi}$ per second. Identify an expression for $P(t),$ the total pressure in the tire at any time $t\\ge 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-49315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p(t)=4t-(t+2)=3t-2$ be the rate at which the pressure in the tire is changing. Note that we subtract $(t+2)$ to indicate the rate at which the pressure is decreasing.\n\nLet $P(T)$ be the pressure in the tire after $T$ seconds. Then, using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t)|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-49315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f7650d9b45cfe48131e292bb92067ed329e52f5d0481fe39a618231e77497d6"} {"question" "q-49315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-49315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+4"] ["math" "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+6"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+2t+6"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+4"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-49315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f7650d9b45cfe48131e292bb92067ed329e52f5d0481fe39a618231e77497d6"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18+9\\pi "] ["math" "23+9\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{36+9\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{45+18\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The bacteria culture in a lab increases at a rate of ${e}^{2t}$ per hour. If, after $2$ hours, there are $2\\,000$ bacteria in the population, approximately how many bacteria are in the population after $6$ hours?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ be the rate at which the number of bacteria is changing and let $S(T)$ be the total number of bacteria and in the population after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+{\\int }_{2}^{T}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}{|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of bacteria in the culture after $T=6$ hours, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(6)=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{12}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}≈83\\,350.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f7650d9b45cfe48131e292bb92067ed329e52f5d0481fe39a618231e77497d6"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "69\\,270"] ["math" "73\\,225"] ["math" "78\\,250"] ["math" "83\\,350"] ["math" "89\\,850"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-103776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "83\\,350"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/1a3b8289936cd2597ac1252e2b28987f245a7c4e88dcf3677e1ba3626460688b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b5becb438f3bc8ab5445e116ddb64bf168013301838231bdc4d32d34283da5f1.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+5=\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f7650d9b45cfe48131e292bb92067ed329e52f5d0481fe39a618231e77497d6"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-156194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{15}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-316254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In $2005$, a group of scientists started tracking the population of sea turtles in a coastal sanctuary. The initial population was $180,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $4+1.5t$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2005.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of sea turtles in the sanctuary at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-316254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{4+1.5t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=t+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of turtles $T$ years after $2005.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(t+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =180+\\frac{1}{2}{T}^{2}+3T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=180+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-316254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f7650d9b45cfe48131e292bb92067ed329e52f5d0481fe39a618231e77497d6"} {"question" "q-316254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"} {"question" "q-316254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180+3t+0.5t^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/state.json" "file_sha256" "967e77794388539168f3a8c76729496d7b51d688e5dfca9024d2d1e3197e6b05"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1306 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
